epictetus-1.md:1: argue/readings: S6 is read 2 ways: epictetus-1.logic.r1 and epictetus-1.logic.r1.2 have "(divine_faithful → O man_faithful) ∧ (divine_free → O man_free) ∧ (divine_beneficent → O man_beneficent) ∧ (divine_magnanimous → O man_magnanimous)" and "imitator_of_god → O act_consistently"; epictetus-1.logic.r1.3 has "divine_faithful → O man_faithful" and "divine_free → O man_free" and "divine_beneficent → O man_beneficent" and "divine_magnanimous → O man_magnanimous" and "imitator_of_god" and "imitator_of_god → O act_consistently"; they differ when only "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man is faithful", "it ought to be that man is free", "it ought to be that man is beneficent", "it ought to be that man is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man does and says everything consistently with being an imitator of God", "it ought to be that man is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man is beneficent", "it ought to be that man is free" and "it ought to be that man is faithful" are true, which the first allows
epictetus-1.md:1: argue/answered: S6: answered no, which keeps epictetus-1.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "the divine is faithful", "the divine is free", "the divine is beneficent", "the divine is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man is faithful", "it ought to be that man is free", "it ought to be that man is beneficent", "it ought to be that man is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man does and says everything consistently with being an imitator of God", "it ought to be that man is faithful", "it ought to be that man is free", "it ought to be that man is beneficent", "it ought to be that man is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man does and says everything consistently with being an imitator of God", "it ought to be that man is magnanimous", "it ought to be that man is beneficent", "it ought to be that man is free" and "it ought to be that man is faithful" are true and "man is an imitator of God" and "it ought to be that man does and says everything consistently with being an imitator of God" are false. Is S6, "If the divine is faithful, man also must be faithful; if it is free, man also must be free; if beneficent, man also must be beneficent; if magnanimous, man also must be magnanimous; as being then an imitator of God he must do and say everything consistently with this fact.", true then?
epictetus-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: epictetus-1.logic.r1 and epictetus-1.logic.r1.2 have "carpenter_by_learning ∧ pilot_by_learning → ¬wish_sufficient ∧ O learn_things"; epictetus-1.logic.r1.3 has nothing from it; they differ when only "the carpenter becomes a carpenter when he has learned certain things", "the pilot becomes a pilot by learning certain things" and "in philosophy it is sufficient to wish to be wise and good" are true, which the second allows
epictetus-1.logic.r1: argue/readings: U1: epictetus-1.logic.r1 and epictetus-1.logic.r1.2 take for granted "if (the carpenter becomes a carpenter when he has learned certain things and the pilot becomes a pilot by learning certain things), then (it is not so that in philosophy it is sufficient to wish to be wise and good and it ought to be that one learns certain things in philosophy)"; epictetus-1.logic.r1.3 does not [in 1 of 2 runs]
epictetus-1.logic.r1: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps epictetus-1.logic.r1 and epictetus-1.logic.r1.2, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if (the carpenter becomes a carpenter when he has learned certain things and the pilot becomes a pilot by learning certain things), then (it is not so that in philosophy it is sufficient to wish to be wise and good and it ought to be that one learns certain things in philosophy)"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument. [in 1 of 2 runs]
epictetus-1.logic.r1: argue/verdicts: valid as epictetus-1.logic.r1, epictetus-1.logic.r1.2 read it, not as the others do: which reading is right decides the verdict [in 1 of 2 runs]
